WEBVTT

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:19.290
RECOGNIZED BY
THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:04.110
NIGHT ON THE GALACTIC RAILROAD

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.260
So, class, as you can see,
it looks like a river,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.350
but it seems to be made of milk.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:29.860
Can anyone here tell me
what gives it this white glow?

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.100
LESSON ON THE STARS

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:50.220
On a clear night,
when you look at the night sky,

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.470
our galaxy is like a great river.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:59.590
So, class, as you can see,
it looks like a river,

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:03.000
but it seems to be made of milk.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.500
Can anyone here tell me
what gives it this white glow?

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:07.500
Me!

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.960
Me! Me! Me! Me!

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.720
Me! Me! Me!

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.010
Giovanni-san.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:22.440
Y- Yes...

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:27.670
Tell me, if you see the galaxy
through a telescope, what is it made of?

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:44.780
Giovanni was sleeping!

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.070
He always does this.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:49.680
He delivers papers every morning.
I feel sorry for him.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.330
- Campanella-san.
- Yes.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.720
What's Campanella doing?

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.090
What's with him?

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:02.760
Did you see Giovanni this morning?

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.340
He was playing with Campanella's dog.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:10.600
Maybe Campanella is ignoring him,
so a dog is the best he can do.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:17.970
All right. Sit down, the both of you.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:23.040
If you were to look at our
pale galaxy through a telescope,

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.530
you would see that it is made of stars.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.780
Right, Giovanni?

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.880
Campanella kept quiet just so...

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.210
Giovanni wouldn't look
stupid in front of the class.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:46.600
But I knew it.
Campanella and I both knew.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:51.940
We had both looked at his father's
Galaxy Atlas in the den of his house.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:55.470
...and if we extend
the comparison further,

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:59.760
all of these stars are like little drops
of oil, floating in that sea of milk.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.280
So if we were to look at this
a little more literally...

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.550
Giovanni's so absentminded lately
because his father hasn't come back yet.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:09.630
But won't his dad be home soon?

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.360
No way! He went out to capture
otters in the North Sea.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.590
But it was an illegal trip,

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:18.370
and they threw him in jail like
a common criminal!

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:20.600
Zanelli!

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.920
I was going to discuss the
tremendous size of the galaxy...

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:31.090
and the various types of stars in it,
but it seems we're out of time for today.

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:34.110
We'll discuss all that in my next lecture.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.960
The Festival of Stars is tonight.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.420
I encourage everyone to take part
in the celebration.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.080
Well, then, class dismissed!

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:55.200
Let's meet by the aspen trees
at the school gate!

00:08:56.010 --> 00:09:00.030
Your father brought this crab
specimen back for us.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.480
Hold it for me, would you?

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.500
Your father also brought back
the antlers and...

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:08.660
those rare fossils
in this display case here.

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:12.440
So, have you heard from
your father recently?

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:13.900
No.

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.500
I see.

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:17.380
Well, don't worry.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:18.960
Yes.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:23.060
Tomorrow I have some errands
to run on your side of town.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.960
I'll probably stop by your house
to say hello to your mother...

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:28.530
and see how she's doing.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:38.140
Sensei. Goodbye.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.260
Yes. Goodbye.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:47.120
I think that your friends are planning to
float lanterns on the river for the festival.

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:49.790
Yeah, but I have to...

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:52.970
Oh, yes. You have to work.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:56.950
But... maybe if I do my work quickly,

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:59.470
I can go to the river and watch afterwards.

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:00.690
You do that.

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:01.940
Yes!

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.960
Yeah! I know how to make them!

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:10.140
Where should we meet?

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:11.170
Under the bridge!

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:12.570
Let's use blue candles!

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:15.570
It'll be easy!
My father showed me how to do it!

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:20.580
- Then you're in charge.
- But do it before the Festival.

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:23.140
We can start at Campanella's
and walk to the town commons!

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:25.140
Can I bring my little sister?

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:34.100
PRINTING HOUSE

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:01.970
Sorry I'm late.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:20.620
Uh... Hello, sir.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.810
Do you have any work for me?

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.000
Can you set this up?

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.510
Yes.

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:19.750
Hey there, shrimp.
Keep up the good work!

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.660
Excuse me, sir. I'm done.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.560
Thank you.

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:10.710
HOME

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:27.550
I can't lose my ticket.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:31.340
If I did, I wouldn't
be able to board the train.

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:47.310
Excuse me...

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:53.320
Sugar and a loaf of bread, please.

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:06.250
There. Loaf of bread and cube sugar.

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:08.020
Thank you.

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:25.390
Here's your change.

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:33.000
Thank you.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.330
Mom. I'm home.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:05.930
How are you feeling?

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:12.080
Oh. Is that you, Giovanni-kun?
I'm glad you're home.

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:15.910
I felt much better today.
The weather was so cool.

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:24.620
I got some sugar to put in your milk.
I thought you'd like that.

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:29.140
When did Sis get home?

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:33.810
Around 3 o'clock.
She did the housework for me.

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:41.180
Mom, didn't they deliver
the milk today?

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:43.620
Isn't it on the counter?

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:46.220
I'll go outside and get it for you.

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:51.290
No, go ahead and eat.
I'll have something later.

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:56.190
Your sister made some tomato soup.
It should be on the table.

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.940
Okay. I'll have a little then.

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:12.000
Say, Mom...

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:24.070
I feel like... Father is going to be
back soon. Don't you?

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:26.660
Yes. I think you're right.

00:19:26.660 --> 00:19:31.120
This morning I read some of
the papers I was delivering.

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:37.550
They said the fishing was
really good in the north this year.

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:43.660
well... Your father
might not have gone fishing.

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:47.950
Of course he did. He wouldn't
have done anything illegal.

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:50.550
You know that he isn't like that.

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:52.670
All those other times, he...

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:59.230
he brought back neat things for the school,
like that crab shell, and the antlers.

00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:04.120
The school still has them on display.

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:12.980
Your father promised to bring you
an otter-skin coat, didn't he?

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:23.570
The kids at school say that.
They're always making fun of me.

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:28.280
Are they bullying you?

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:33.040
Not Campanella.
He doesn't bother me at all.

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:38.960
Campanella's father and your father...

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:42.600
have been friends
since they were very young.

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:48.000
Father used to take me over to
Campanella's house to play sometimes.

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:54.040
That was so much fun...

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:03.700
You know Campanella has a wonderful
toy steam train that runs on alcohol.

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:15.090
Isn't the Festival of Stars tonight?

00:21:17.090 --> 00:21:22.100
Yeah. I'm going to the festival after
I go out to get your milk from the dairy.

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:24.470
Good. I'm glad you're going.

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:26.600
But don't go near the river.

00:21:27.780 --> 00:21:31.440
I'll watch from the shore.
I'll be back in an hour or so.

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.250
Take your time.

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:37.170
I won't worry if you're going to be
with Campanella-san.

00:21:38.090 --> 00:21:39.880
Oh yeah, I'll be with him.

00:21:41.990 --> 00:21:45.240
See you. It might actually be
an hour and a half.

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:55.070
THE NIGHT OF THE CENTAURUS FESTIVAL

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:07.230
Zanelli!

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:11.400
Are you going to the town commons?

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:18.330
Giovanni, isn't your father
going to bring you an otter-skin coat?

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:58.880
Zanelli... He's such an idiot!

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:04.350
Some people see the galaxy
as a giant river of milk...

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:07.070
and all of these stars are
like drops of oil...

00:25:07.070 --> 00:25:10.200
...floating in that sea of milk.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:13.410
Here, where we live,
exists as the stars do.

00:25:13.410 --> 00:25:16.470
Our sun and the Earth itself
float within this sea.

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:21.140
You could even say that we all live in
the waters of the river of heaven...

00:25:28.550 --> 00:25:33.020
- The Centaurus Festival!
- Centaurus Festival!

00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:42.390
Oh, yeah.
I almost forgot to get Mom's milk.

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:38.140
Good evening?

00:27:43.790 --> 00:27:45.510
Good evening?

00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:01.490
Um, I'm sorry to bother you
but we didn't get our milk today,

00:28:01.490 --> 00:28:03.000
so I came to get it.

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:11.210
So... I came here to pick up our milk.

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:16.430
No one's here right now.

00:28:16.430 --> 00:28:19.200
Come back tomorrow.

00:28:20.220 --> 00:28:23.220
I'm sorry, but I really need
to get it tonight!

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:27.680
What's the rush?
Are you going to miss your train?

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:32.840
Uh, no. You see, my mother's sick,
and she really needs it tonight.

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:36.810
I see. Well, come back a little later.

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:57.950
CROSS ON THE TOWN COMMONS

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:46.910
Hey, Giovanni! I can't wait to see
your new otter-skin coat!

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:49.410
We can't WAIT to see your new
otter-skin coat!

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:52.790
We can't WAIT to see your new
otter-skin coat!

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:21.040
Oh, my. What's wrong?

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:09.500
THE WEATHER STATION PILLAR

00:34:03.830 --> 00:34:09.760
Every light up there in that
glowing band is actually a star.

00:35:38.930 --> 00:35:44.950
Galaxy Station! Galaxy Station!

00:35:46.600 --> 00:35:49.570
THE GALACTIC STATION

00:36:45.530 --> 00:36:47.300
Campanella!

00:36:49.800 --> 00:36:54.720
The others all ran after me,
but they were just too late.

00:36:55.870 --> 00:36:59.740
Even Zanelli.
He ran as fast as he could...

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:01.510
but he just wasn't fast enough.

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:03.980
So, are they waiting for you somewhere?

00:37:03.980 --> 00:37:06.040
Zanelli's already gone home.

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:10.840
His father came by to get him.

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:16.890
What's wrong?

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:20.530
Your coat is all wet. You look like
you're covered with dew or something.

00:37:21.230 --> 00:37:24.930
Darn it!
I must have dropped my canteen.

00:37:25.670 --> 00:37:27.510
But that's okay.

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:30.940
Swan Station is coming up soon.

00:37:30.940 --> 00:37:33.530
I love to watch swans.

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:38.450
I can spot them from miles away,
as they glide across the river.

00:37:38.450 --> 00:37:42.070
What a neat map!
Where did you get it?

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:45.920
It looks like it's made out of obsidian!

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:50.470
I got it at the Galaxy Station.
Didn't they give you one there too?

00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:55.540
I don't really remember
being at any Galaxy Station.

00:37:57.030 --> 00:38:00.500
Hey, this spot is
where we are right now, isn't it?

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:02.300
Yeah.

00:38:07.440 --> 00:38:10.750
The riverbed is
shining in the moonlight.

00:38:19.460 --> 00:38:23.170
That's not moonlight!
It's the glow of the galaxy.

00:38:32.440 --> 00:38:33.880
What is it?

00:38:33.880 --> 00:38:37.710
I didn't think we'd get a chance
to go off on our own tonight.

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:39.660
I mean, did you?

00:38:39.660 --> 00:38:42.930
What do you mean? You thought
we'd be with everyone from school?

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:45.400
No, not that. Forget it.

00:38:49.490 --> 00:38:52.990
Look, those must be
the fields of heaven outside.

00:39:12.080 --> 00:39:15.810
This train doesn't burn coal, does it?

00:39:15.810 --> 00:39:18.390
It must run on alcohol.

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:22.510
Look, the gentian flowers are blooming.

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:27.850
It's finally autumn...

00:39:58.260 --> 00:40:00.890
I'm going to jump out
and pick some of them!

00:40:00.890 --> 00:40:02.980
I promise I'll be right back, okay?

00:40:04.090 --> 00:40:08.460
It's too late.
We've already left them far behind.

00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:24.340
THE NORTHERN CROSS

00:40:45.670 --> 00:40:47.000
Hey...

00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:50.730
I hope mom forgives me for this.

00:41:06.660 --> 00:41:12.650
Oh, yeah, mom!
She must be very far away by now.

00:41:12.960 --> 00:41:15.880
I'll bet she's thinking about me.

00:41:20.070 --> 00:41:25.850
You know, I would do anything
in the world to make my mother happy.

00:41:25.850 --> 00:41:30.670
But sometimes I wonder if there's something
I could do to make her truly happy?

00:41:31.180 --> 00:41:33.650
What? I'm sure your mother is happy.

00:41:33.650 --> 00:41:35.920
Why do you think she wouldn't be?

00:41:36.350 --> 00:41:38.510
I don't know how to explain it...

00:41:39.190 --> 00:41:44.330
But I think people are happiest
when they do something truly good.

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:48.320
That's why I think my mother
will forgive me for what I've done.

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:13.550
THE PLIOCENE COAST

00:43:18.060 --> 00:43:20.630
It looks like
we'll be at Swan Station soon!

00:43:20.630 --> 00:43:23.280
We'll arrive at exactly 11 o'clock.

00:43:42.550 --> 00:43:45.100
Hey, do you think we
should go outside and look around?

00:44:02.570 --> 00:44:04.620
Let's walk around a little bit.

00:44:10.370 --> 00:44:12.630
What a huge station!

00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:28.980
Where'd everyone go?
No one's here.

00:44:30.890 --> 00:44:32.990
You're right.

00:44:57.890 --> 00:44:59.820
Which way do you want to go?

00:44:59.820 --> 00:45:01.460
Straight ahead.

00:45:01.460 --> 00:45:03.520
Okay! I'll race you!

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:13.740
What is it?

00:45:13.740 --> 00:45:16.010
There's a tiny door there.

00:45:17.070 --> 00:45:18.810
We should try opening it.

00:45:18.810 --> 00:45:20.500
Why not?

00:45:32.690 --> 00:45:36.540
It goes pretty far down.
Think we should try it?

00:45:36.540 --> 00:45:37.960
Let's do it.

00:45:46.300 --> 00:45:48.390
It's a town commons.

00:45:59.400 --> 00:46:01.400
Everyone's gone from here too.

00:46:02.590 --> 00:46:04.410
Looks like it.

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:07.320
Something's written over there.

00:46:14.100 --> 00:46:18.140
"This way to the Pliocene Coast."
Let's see!

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:27.610
They look just like the stone steps
that go down to the river.

00:46:41.420 --> 00:46:44.640
How far down do you think it goes?

00:46:45.600 --> 00:46:47.760
I don't know.

00:47:34.080 --> 00:47:37.470
Every grain of sand is
a crystalline jewel.

00:47:39.850 --> 00:47:43.510
There's a spark
of fire inside every one.

00:47:52.660 --> 00:47:54.700
Someone's up ahead of us!

00:47:56.100 --> 00:47:57.890
Let's go and have a look.

00:48:26.060 --> 00:48:27.630
What is it?

00:48:27.630 --> 00:48:31.390
It's a walnut.
Just look! They're everywhere.

00:48:35.840 --> 00:48:38.650
That's a huge one!

00:48:38.650 --> 00:48:41.190
It's twice as big as a normal walnut.

00:48:41.190 --> 00:48:43.810
And it looks in perfect shape!

00:48:48.690 --> 00:48:50.570
Look over there! It's them.

00:48:51.190 --> 00:48:53.390
I think they're digging something up.

00:49:17.680 --> 00:49:20.420
Remember, be careful
near that outcropping!

00:49:20.420 --> 00:49:25.090
Use the shovel!
You'd better dig a little farther back!

00:49:25.090 --> 00:49:28.400
Careful, careful!
Try to work gently!

00:49:39.200 --> 00:49:41.220
Are you here to observe?

00:49:41.220 --> 00:49:43.090
Yes.

00:49:43.090 --> 00:49:45.740
I see there that you have
found yourself a walnut!

00:49:45.740 --> 00:49:47.640
There were millions of them, once.

00:49:47.640 --> 00:49:51.050
That walnut dates from
1.2 million years ago.

00:49:51.050 --> 00:49:53.540
1.2 million years!!!

00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:56.940
We've dated these rocks.

00:49:56.940 --> 00:50:00.040
They come from the Tertiary Era,
1.2 million years ago.

00:50:00.860 --> 00:50:04.510
Be careful with that chisel!
Not so hard!

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:08.060
We've found an
ancient animal called a vos,

00:50:08.060 --> 00:50:12.370
which was the
ancestor of our modern cow.

00:50:12.370 --> 00:50:14.040
Will you put it on display?

00:50:14.040 --> 00:50:17.070
No, this is pure research.

00:50:18.210 --> 00:50:22.550
From here, this looks like a thick,
rich, fossil stratum.

00:50:22.550 --> 00:50:28.050
The evidence indicates that
the rocks are 1.2 million years old.

00:50:28.050 --> 00:50:31.790
But to others, this region might
look completely different.

00:50:31.790 --> 00:50:34.510
It all depends on
who might be looking at it.

00:50:37.460 --> 00:50:42.710
To someone else, this whole area
might appear to be air, or even water...

00:50:42.710 --> 00:50:45.150
or an empty patch of sky.

00:50:48.520 --> 00:50:51.530
Do you understand? Still...

00:50:51.880 --> 00:50:54.940
Hey, be careful!
Don't use the shovel there!

00:50:54.940 --> 00:50:56.720
That ground is too soft!

00:50:56.720 --> 00:50:59.040
Yes! Gotcha!

00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:03.670
It's time to go!

00:51:03.670 --> 00:51:05.890
But I want to see a little more.

00:51:05.890 --> 00:51:08.710
We can't.
We'll miss the train if we stay.

00:51:08.710 --> 00:51:10.140
Let's go!

00:51:10.530 --> 00:51:12.060
Thanks a lot!

00:51:12.060 --> 00:51:14.520
Very well then. Goodbye.

00:51:38.250 --> 00:51:40.930
Huh? The whole town has changed.

00:51:45.130 --> 00:51:47.410
Everything turned to stone.

00:51:48.750 --> 00:51:50.340
Hurry!

00:52:27.370 --> 00:52:30.840
You and I just ran as fast as the wind...

00:52:30.840 --> 00:52:33.440
for 1.2 million years!

00:52:33.440 --> 00:52:35.060
It's amazing.

00:52:36.110 --> 00:52:39.400
Yeah, for 1.2 million years!

00:53:16.120 --> 00:53:19.080
BIRDCATCHER

00:53:24.190 --> 00:53:27.180
Is it all right if I sit here?

00:53:27.560 --> 00:53:29.690
Uh... Yeah... sure.

00:53:51.220 --> 00:53:53.730
Thank you very much, young man.

00:54:32.560 --> 00:54:36.430
Where is it you two boys
are headed off to?

00:54:37.200 --> 00:54:39.250
To the end of the universe!

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:41.620
I see. That's wonderful.

00:54:41.620 --> 00:54:45.380
You know, this train can actually
take you there.

00:54:45.380 --> 00:54:47.810
And where is it
that you're heading to?

00:54:47.810 --> 00:54:53.080
The next stop.
I'm a birdcatcher so I get off next.

00:54:53.080 --> 00:54:55.900
What do you do with the birds
after you catch them?

00:54:55.900 --> 00:54:59.800
You eat them.
Why, haven't you ever eaten a bird?

00:55:13.800 --> 00:55:17.290
Here, look at these.
I just caught these herons yesterday.

00:55:22.280 --> 00:55:24.450
And you really eat them?

00:55:24.450 --> 00:55:27.920
Sure. I get orders for them every day.

00:55:27.920 --> 00:55:30.450
Who in the world could eat a heron?

00:55:30.450 --> 00:55:35.480
Everyone eats them!
Here, why don't you try some yourself?

00:55:42.770 --> 00:55:44.810
Here you go.

00:55:46.540 --> 00:55:48.990
And some for you, too.

00:55:56.980 --> 00:55:59.000
Please, go ahead, eat some.

00:56:05.050 --> 00:56:06.590
Go ahead.

00:56:12.030 --> 00:56:14.340
This is... It's candy!

00:56:17.900 --> 00:56:20.140
Why don't you try it, too?

00:56:20.140 --> 00:56:22.390
Sure. Thanks a lot.

00:56:22.910 --> 00:56:25.770
What magnificent herons!

00:56:26.510 --> 00:56:29.090
Thank you very much.

00:56:29.090 --> 00:56:33.220
So, tell me, do the migratory flocks
look promising this year or not?

00:56:33.220 --> 00:56:35.720
Oh, yes! They're incredible!

00:56:35.720 --> 00:56:38.450
There are hordes of birds
coming in from all over!

00:56:38.450 --> 00:56:42.560
Just a couple of days ago,
I got some complaint calls.

00:56:42.560 --> 00:56:47.600
People were calling to tell me that
I shouldn't blink the lighthouse light.

00:56:47.600 --> 00:56:49.930
I told them I wasn't
doing any such thing.

00:56:49.930 --> 00:56:56.240
There were huge flocks of birds in front
of the light, one right after another!

00:56:56.240 --> 00:56:58.140
I told those idiots,

00:56:58.140 --> 00:57:01.640
"If you wanna complain, then complain
to the big one who leads the flock."

00:57:01.640 --> 00:57:07.880
"He's easy to spot: long legs,
a huge beak, and a rustling cape!"

00:57:07.880 --> 00:57:10.210
Yeah, that's what I told them.

00:57:13.690 --> 00:57:17.400
Ah, yes. This is my stop.

00:57:33.740 --> 00:57:36.850
Hey, he's out there!

01:00:01.020 --> 01:00:03.750
Ah, that was refreshing!

01:00:04.530 --> 01:00:10.410
There's really nothing more satisfying
than hard work at a job you do well.

01:00:14.600 --> 01:00:17.770
You have geese and heron.
Can you get cranes?

01:00:17.770 --> 01:00:20.910
Sure, there's plenty of them all around.

01:00:20.910 --> 01:00:23.910
You can hear them now. Go on.

01:00:23.910 --> 01:00:27.000
Just listen carefully
and you'll hear them.

01:00:36.950 --> 01:00:39.890
BLIND RADIO TELEGRAPHIST

01:01:12.060 --> 01:01:14.640
It's the wireless operator.

01:01:46.830 --> 01:01:48.840
Thank you very much.

01:01:48.840 --> 01:01:51.310
Here, take my hand.

01:02:18.230 --> 01:02:19.600
Is it here?

01:02:19.600 --> 01:02:22.180
Yes. Thank you.

01:03:10.650 --> 01:03:13.310
Excuse me, but do you hear anything?

01:03:17.790 --> 01:03:19.690
What are you hearing?

01:03:19.690 --> 01:03:21.760
I don't know.

01:03:21.760 --> 01:03:25.640
I've been picking this up
for a while now,

01:03:25.640 --> 01:03:27.610
but I can't seem to decipher it.

01:03:27.610 --> 01:03:30.150
What does it sound like?

01:03:31.800 --> 01:03:34.800
Can you take dictation for me?

01:03:34.800 --> 01:03:36.160
Sure.

01:03:43.180 --> 01:03:46.680
The static is pretty bad.

01:03:47.480 --> 01:03:52.620
"...God... to... Thee... near..."

01:03:52.620 --> 01:03:55.140
There's more static.

01:03:55.140 --> 01:04:02.490
"E... ven... though... it... be... a... cross..."

01:04:02.490 --> 01:04:06.840
"...eth... me... still... all..."

01:04:06.840 --> 01:04:12.860
"...my... song... shall... be..."

01:04:13.860 --> 01:04:16.210
Interesting...
That's all there is.

01:04:16.210 --> 01:04:19.420
Nothing but static after that.

01:04:24.320 --> 01:04:25.590
What is it?

01:04:25.590 --> 01:04:31.930
The signal just keeps repeating over
and over again. Always the same.

01:04:31.930 --> 01:04:35.060
I can pick it up on the wireless.

01:04:36.370 --> 01:04:41.740
...God to Thee near
even though it be a cross...

01:04:41.740 --> 01:04:46.730
...eth me still
all my song shall be...

01:04:50.200 --> 01:04:53.140
THE OBSERVATORY OF ALBIREO

01:04:57.350 --> 01:04:59.190
So what do you think it means?

01:04:59.190 --> 01:05:00.830
I don't know.

01:05:00.830 --> 01:05:03.650
Number 306.

01:05:06.300 --> 01:05:09.920
It's number 306.

01:05:10.730 --> 01:05:12.940
Number 306?

01:05:13.440 --> 01:05:17.030
Hymn number 306.

01:05:17.670 --> 01:05:26.900
Nearer, my God, to Thee, nearer to Thee!

01:05:27.280 --> 01:05:35.910
Even though it be a cross that raiseth me;

01:05:36.790 --> 01:05:47.890
Still all my song would be
nearer, my God, to Thee,

01:05:48.300 --> 01:05:57.420
Nearer, my God, to Thee, nearer to Thee!

01:05:59.210 --> 01:06:04.050
It's a prayer to be
carried away to Heaven.

01:06:04.890 --> 01:06:07.470
Whose prayer?

01:06:07.470 --> 01:06:09.160
I don't know.

01:06:09.160 --> 01:06:13.230
But they probably know,
in the observatory at Albireo.

01:06:13.230 --> 01:06:15.730
The observatory at Albireo?

01:06:15.730 --> 01:06:19.980
We're already leaving the Swan.

01:06:21.300 --> 01:06:27.000
You can see the observatory
at Albireo outside right now.

01:06:28.380 --> 01:06:33.430
Of course, they probably won't
give you an answer.

01:07:32.140 --> 01:07:41.200
Nearer, my God, to Thee, nearer to Thee!

01:07:41.580 --> 01:07:50.830
Even though it be a cross that raiseth me;

01:07:51.460 --> 01:08:02.050
Still all my song would be
nearer, my God, to Thee,

01:08:02.050 --> 01:08:11.620
Nearer, my God, to Thee, nearer to Thee!

01:08:12.010 --> 01:08:15.010
GIOVANNI'S TICKET

01:08:26.800 --> 01:08:29.130
Ticket inspection!

01:08:29.130 --> 01:08:31.550
Ticket inspection!
It's time for ticket inspection!

01:08:33.600 --> 01:08:36.050
May I have your tickets, please?

01:09:13.240 --> 01:09:14.950
Here.

01:09:20.850 --> 01:09:23.130
Your ticket, please.

01:10:05.380 --> 01:10:09.680
Excuse me, sir, but did you get
this ticket in the third dimension?

01:10:10.390 --> 01:10:13.100
I'm not really sure where I got it...

01:10:13.100 --> 01:10:15.520
Very well.

01:10:16.340 --> 01:10:21.040
We will reach the Southern Cross
at precisely 3 o'clock.

01:10:28.850 --> 01:10:31.370
My ticket is different.

01:10:31.850 --> 01:10:35.850
Can my ticket take me
where you're going?

01:10:35.850 --> 01:10:39.050
Of course!
That's an incredibly rare ticket!

01:10:39.050 --> 01:10:42.930
It can even take you... Yes,
it can take you to the true Heaven!

01:10:42.930 --> 01:10:45.470
Why, with that ticket...

01:10:45.470 --> 01:10:50.710
Yes, I see! It'll take you
to everywhere this train runs.

01:10:50.710 --> 01:10:53.950
You must be very special indeed!

01:10:54.400 --> 01:10:56.400
I don't understand.

01:10:56.400 --> 01:10:58.620
Are you maybe?

01:10:58.620 --> 01:11:00.150
Yes, of course.

01:11:00.150 --> 01:11:03.620
I knew you were different
from everyone else here!

01:11:03.620 --> 01:11:07.990
That's it, isn't it?
You're... Still, I can't believe it!

01:11:07.990 --> 01:11:11.630
I've never seen a ticket
like that before!

01:11:11.630 --> 01:11:14.780
Eagle Station's coming up soon.

01:11:15.900 --> 01:11:19.220
Hey... No matter where we go,
we'll be together, right?

01:11:21.400 --> 01:11:23.850
Where did that man go?

01:11:43.730 --> 01:11:45.950
Where did he go?

01:11:51.800 --> 01:11:56.300
I sorta wish now that I had spent
more time talking to him.

01:11:58.070 --> 01:12:02.100
It kind of felt like
he was interrupting at first.

01:12:02.580 --> 01:12:04.800
But now I feel bad.

01:12:07.080 --> 01:12:09.120
Yeah. Me, too.

01:12:18.230 --> 01:12:21.180
APPLES

01:12:39.410 --> 01:12:42.580
All of a sudden, I smell apples.

01:12:42.580 --> 01:12:45.430
Yeah, you're right.
I smell them, too.

01:13:04.240 --> 01:13:06.060
Thank you.

01:13:17.990 --> 01:13:21.530
The ship we were on hit
a huge iceberg and sank.

01:13:21.530 --> 01:13:24.620
I was hired as a tutor
to these children.

01:13:24.980 --> 01:13:29.330
Two months ago, their father was
called home on business.

01:13:29.330 --> 01:13:32.010
He left his daughter and son in my care.

01:13:32.010 --> 01:13:34.710
We were on our way home to join him.

01:13:43.880 --> 01:13:47.880
It was late at night, and we could
barely see the moonlight.

01:13:47.880 --> 01:13:50.930
The fog was incredibly thick.

01:14:40.470 --> 01:14:42.940
Then our ship hit an iceberg,

01:14:42.940 --> 01:14:45.870
and the decks filled with water.

01:14:45.870 --> 01:14:50.710
The port side lifeboats were
already underwater,

01:14:50.710 --> 01:14:54.110
so there weren't enough boats
to go around.

01:14:57.520 --> 01:15:01.460
The people around us quickly stepped
aside to open a path and let us through.

01:15:01.460 --> 01:15:07.690
But it was clear that there were
far too many elderly and children...

01:15:07.690 --> 01:15:11.010
for the boats that were left.

01:15:11.370 --> 01:15:15.780
I'm responsible for these two children.
Their father left them in my care.

01:15:15.780 --> 01:15:17.970
I am their tutor...

01:15:17.970 --> 01:15:22.060
and he would want me to try and save
them, so I began to push forward.

01:15:22.810 --> 01:15:26.750
But I thought about what would
bring them true happiness.

01:15:26.750 --> 01:15:30.650
What if I could truly save them,
even if it cost them their lives?

01:15:30.650 --> 01:15:34.310
Allowing us to die would be
my sin alone.

01:15:34.310 --> 01:15:38.080
The best thing for them
might be to go now, together...

01:15:38.080 --> 01:15:42.930
...to be with God.
Better than to be saved this way.

01:15:42.930 --> 01:15:47.010
But I remembered that it was my duty
to save their lives, so I pushed ahead.

01:15:48.200 --> 01:15:53.380
I started shouting for them to
get these children into the lifeboats.

01:15:53.380 --> 01:15:56.710
But as I saw mothers say goodbye
to their children forever...

01:15:56.710 --> 01:16:03.250
...and as I saw fathers stand silently by,
desperately trying to hold back tears...

01:16:03.250 --> 01:16:06.600
...I made my decision.

01:16:06.600 --> 01:16:11.130
I decided that we would float
together as long as possible.

01:16:11.130 --> 01:16:14.750
So I held them, and the three of us
waited for the ship to sink.

01:16:41.150 --> 01:16:44.890
I'm not sure what happiness really is.

01:16:44.890 --> 01:16:48.440
But if you keep to the true path,
even under terrible conditions,

01:16:48.440 --> 01:16:52.600
you can climb any mountain,
no matter how high.

01:16:52.600 --> 01:16:57.940
I believe we can all reach true
happiness, one step at a time.

01:16:59.110 --> 01:17:01.310
Yes. I agree.

01:17:01.310 --> 01:17:04.980
To reach true happiness,
we must serve God...

01:17:04.980 --> 01:17:08.970
even if we do so from
the depths of our despair.

01:17:40.150 --> 01:17:42.860
Would you care for an apple?

01:17:46.960 --> 01:17:49.500
Oh, it's so beautiful.

01:17:49.500 --> 01:17:51.740
Please, go ahead and have some.

01:17:56.230 --> 01:17:59.030
Would you care for an apple?
Let's share.

01:18:07.710 --> 01:18:09.560
Thank you.

01:18:14.680 --> 01:18:16.500
Thank you.

01:18:17.220 --> 01:18:20.580
Where do these
delicious apples come from?

01:18:20.580 --> 01:18:26.180
Around here, things just seem to
grow on their own, without effort.

01:18:26.180 --> 01:18:28.730
It isn't hard at all.

01:18:28.730 --> 01:18:34.400
If you sow the proper seeds, you'll find
they bloom quickly all by themselves.

01:18:34.400 --> 01:18:36.270
I see.

01:18:36.270 --> 01:18:40.190
It's true for apples
and even for candy...

01:18:48.650 --> 01:18:52.690
I was dreaming.
I was dreaming about my mother.

01:18:52.690 --> 01:18:56.500
She was just asking me
if I wanted a nice big red apple.

01:18:57.130 --> 01:19:00.720
And when I woke up,
I was holding one!

01:19:02.260 --> 01:19:06.470
Oh, we're still on the train.

01:19:06.470 --> 01:19:08.720
What a beautiful apple!

01:19:13.110 --> 01:19:15.180
It smells delicious.

01:19:15.180 --> 01:19:19.230
I must have smelled it in my sleep.

01:19:48.210 --> 01:19:50.550
Look, crows!

01:19:50.880 --> 01:19:52.680
There's a whole bunch of them outside!

01:19:52.680 --> 01:19:56.190
Those aren't crows.
They're magpies, actually.

01:19:56.890 --> 01:19:58.740
Oh, I see...

01:20:03.420 --> 01:20:05.660
Wow! Look at them all...

01:20:24.410 --> 01:20:26.240
Look! More apples!

01:20:54.610 --> 01:20:56.850
That's hymn 306.

01:20:56.850 --> 01:21:00.490
Those people in the forest out
there are singing in chorus.

01:21:17.880 --> 01:21:20.800
SYMPHONY OF THE NEW WORLD

01:22:16.390 --> 01:22:18.840
Look! It's a field of corn!

01:22:22.430 --> 01:22:24.170
Yeah, it is.

01:22:32.640 --> 01:22:35.250
There, it's the station.

01:23:29.900 --> 01:23:32.750
That sounds like the New World Symphony.

01:24:54.050 --> 01:24:55.450
What is it?

01:24:55.450 --> 01:24:58.790
There was a boy outside
standing in front of a small house.

01:24:58.790 --> 01:25:00.290
Really?

01:25:01.860 --> 01:25:05.200
I think I know that boy.

01:25:05.860 --> 01:25:07.900
I know him, too.

01:25:08.260 --> 01:25:11.290
Then we must have met him
somewhere before.

01:25:24.960 --> 01:25:27.880
THE FLAME OF SCORPIO

01:25:29.280 --> 01:25:32.250
We're in this region now, aren't we?

01:25:32.250 --> 01:25:33.850
That's right.

01:25:37.130 --> 01:25:39.760
Huh? Is that on fire?

01:25:43.480 --> 01:25:46.130
That's the Flame of Scorpio.

01:25:46.130 --> 01:25:49.060
I know all about that Flame.

01:25:49.060 --> 01:25:51.490
What's the Flame of Scorpio?

01:25:53.170 --> 01:25:55.660
It's a scorpion that burned to death.

01:25:55.660 --> 01:25:58.670
It was so hot...
it is still burning today.

01:25:59.410 --> 01:26:01.950
Scorpions are really bad bugs!

01:26:02.450 --> 01:26:03.830
My teacher told me that...

01:26:03.830 --> 01:26:07.030
they'll try to sting you with
their tail and you can die!

01:26:07.030 --> 01:26:10.320
That's true, but scorpions aren't bad.

01:26:10.320 --> 01:26:15.060
Our father told us once about
the scorpion of Baldora Field.

01:26:15.060 --> 01:26:18.530
The scorpion killed other bugs
and ate them.

01:26:18.530 --> 01:26:21.670
Then one day, a weasel found him.

01:26:21.670 --> 01:26:26.170
The scorpion fled,
and fell into a deep well.

01:26:26.170 --> 01:26:33.430
He thought to himself,
"How often have I eaten other creatures?"

01:26:34.520 --> 01:26:39.870
"And now, the one time that
I was the prey, I fled in utter terror."

01:26:40.450 --> 01:26:45.870
"And look what came of that.
I'll die in this well, alone."

01:26:47.630 --> 01:26:50.810
"Ah... Life is filled with uncertainty."

01:26:50.810 --> 01:26:56.330
"I could have given the weasel
another day of life with my sacrifice."

01:26:57.210 --> 01:26:59.690
"But now..."

01:26:59.690 --> 01:27:03.250
"my death will help no one.
I am useless."

01:27:03.880 --> 01:27:07.900
"Dear Lord, I beg of you, look into
my heart and hear my prayer."

01:27:07.900 --> 01:27:10.920
"In my next life, don't let me
waste myself."

01:27:10.920 --> 01:27:18.670
"Let me use my body for the true
happiness of everyone in the world."

01:27:21.330 --> 01:27:28.260
And then the scorpion burst into flame.
A brilliant crimson glow.

01:27:29.070 --> 01:27:32.730
And by the light of his burning body,
he lit up the night forever.

01:28:12.050 --> 01:28:15.000
THE SOUTHERN CROSS

01:28:19.590 --> 01:28:21.550
How beautiful.

01:28:32.230 --> 01:28:34.910
We're at the village of Centaurus!

01:28:35.070 --> 01:28:37.240
Centaurus! The Festival!

01:28:37.240 --> 01:28:41.300
Yeah! You're right!
It's the Festival of Stars!

01:28:41.880 --> 01:28:46.180
It looks like everyone is out celebrating
the Centaurus Festival tonight.

01:29:01.830 --> 01:29:06.210
We'll be at the Southern Cross soon.
Get ready to disembark.

01:29:07.340 --> 01:29:09.970
We'll be at the Southern Cross soon.

01:29:09.970 --> 01:29:11.770
We need to get ready to go now.

01:29:11.770 --> 01:29:14.830
I want to stay here on the train!

01:29:14.830 --> 01:29:17.310
I know, but we have to get off here.

01:29:17.310 --> 01:29:21.320
No! I'm staying on the train!
I want to ride some more!

01:29:21.320 --> 01:29:25.620
I'm sorry but we have to get off here.

01:29:25.620 --> 01:29:28.810
This is where we get to Heaven.

01:29:56.680 --> 01:29:58.410
Come, we need to go.

01:30:12.400 --> 01:30:15.010
Well... Goodbye, then.

01:30:15.570 --> 01:30:17.200
Goodbye.

01:30:17.200 --> 01:30:19.120
Goodbye.

01:31:42.660 --> 01:31:45.570
I guess... It's just the two of us now.

01:31:51.030 --> 01:31:54.860
Hey, let's just keep
going like this forever, okay?

01:31:54.860 --> 01:31:58.410
That's right!
We'll always be together, won't we?

01:32:01.180 --> 01:32:03.860
Hmm... I'd like that.

01:32:12.820 --> 01:32:16.290
But then again,
where exactly are we going, anyway?

01:32:16.660 --> 01:32:19.540
Well, I... I don't know.

01:32:19.540 --> 01:32:21.980
As long as we're together!

01:32:36.310 --> 01:32:39.270
THE COAL SACK

01:32:40.080 --> 01:32:41.950
Hey! Look at that!

01:32:49.620 --> 01:32:53.380
It's the Coal Sack. A hole in the sky.

01:32:58.770 --> 01:33:04.910
Hey, don't worry!
I'm not afraid of the dark anymore.

01:33:04.910 --> 01:33:07.860
We'll get through
this together, won't we?

01:33:09.380 --> 01:33:12.630
Yeah, I guess you're right then.

01:33:15.920 --> 01:33:19.290
Look at that lovely field out there.

01:33:19.720 --> 01:33:21.390
Where?

01:33:26.990 --> 01:33:30.080
That must be the field
of the true Heaven.

01:33:39.970 --> 01:33:43.080
I see now. That's where my mother is.

01:34:21.750 --> 01:34:26.310
Campanella, we'll always
be together, won't we?

01:34:28.820 --> 01:34:30.810
Campanella!

01:34:36.520 --> 01:34:40.190
Campanella!
Wait! I'm going with you!

01:34:43.970 --> 01:34:46.110
Goodbye, Giovanni.

01:34:48.880 --> 01:34:50.550
Wait!

01:34:50.550 --> 01:34:53.930
You said we'd be together forever!
You promised!

01:34:58.790 --> 01:35:03.340
Open up! Open up!
Campanella! Campanella!

01:35:03.820 --> 01:35:08.350
Open up! Open up!
Campanella!

01:35:10.360 --> 01:35:13.720
Goodbye, Giovanni...

01:35:15.720 --> 01:35:17.270
Campanella...

01:36:32.030 --> 01:36:35.800
Campanella!

01:36:56.020 --> 01:36:58.990
BLACK RIVER

01:37:35.410 --> 01:37:40.160
Oh, yeah!
Mom is still waiting to have dinner.

01:38:04.040 --> 01:38:05.900
Good evening.

01:38:05.900 --> 01:38:07.980
Yes?

01:38:07.980 --> 01:38:11.710
Excuse me,
but we didn't get our milk today.

01:38:11.710 --> 01:38:13.950
Oh, I'm terribly sorry.

01:38:13.950 --> 01:38:17.550
I'm afraid I left the calf's gate
open this afternoon.

01:38:17.550 --> 01:38:22.200
He went straight for his mother and drank
half her milk before I could stop him!

01:38:22.200 --> 01:38:23.560
Here.

01:38:23.560 --> 01:38:26.560
Oh, I see. Thank you very much.

01:38:27.060 --> 01:38:30.050
- I'm very sorry.
- It's all right.

01:38:59.230 --> 01:39:01.250
Marceau, what's wrong?

01:39:01.250 --> 01:39:05.400
Giovanni, hurry!
Everybody's all down by the river!

01:39:05.400 --> 01:39:06.470
The river?

01:39:06.470 --> 01:39:09.990
Giovanni...
Campanella fell in the river!

01:39:10.700 --> 01:39:13.680
Zanelli was standing in
one of the boats....

01:39:13.680 --> 01:39:17.040
and he leaned out to
put his lantern in the water...

01:39:17.040 --> 01:39:20.630
but the boat lurched and Zanelli
fell in and started to sink!

01:39:20.630 --> 01:39:23.380
Campanella dove right in after him!

01:39:23.380 --> 01:39:26.450
He swam down and pushed
Zanelli back to the boat!

01:39:26.450 --> 01:39:30.230
Kato grabbed Zanelli and pulled
him back in, but by then...

01:39:30.890 --> 01:39:33.990
By then, Campanella had disappeared!

01:39:33.990 --> 01:39:38.000
Everyone in town is searching for him.
His father's there, too.

01:39:38.000 --> 01:39:42.790
But they haven't found him!
They took Zanelli home, already.

01:40:59.080 --> 01:41:03.440
Well... He may be further
downstream by now.

01:41:08.220 --> 01:41:13.080
No. It's no use now.
It's been 45 minutes since he fell in.

01:41:15.000 --> 01:41:17.070
In that case...

01:41:47.460 --> 01:41:49.540
Campanella...

01:41:52.000 --> 01:41:55.280
Giovanni-san, I didn't see you there.

01:41:55.280 --> 01:41:58.660
I want to thank you very much
for coming here tonight.

01:41:59.770 --> 01:42:03.540
I've been meaning to ask you, has your
father returned home from the north yet?

01:42:03.540 --> 01:42:05.390
No.

01:42:05.750 --> 01:42:07.210
I wonder what's wrong?

01:42:07.210 --> 01:42:11.210
He seemed very happy in the letter
I got from him just a few days ago.

01:42:13.090 --> 01:42:15.660
He should have been home today.

01:42:15.660 --> 01:42:18.270
Maybe his boat is late.

01:42:20.130 --> 01:42:23.110
Giovanni-san, if you would please...

01:42:23.110 --> 01:42:26.990
bring your classmates over to
my house after school tomorrow.

01:42:34.170 --> 01:42:38.160
I know Campanella is out there.

01:42:38.160 --> 01:42:41.440
He's there, at the edge of the universe.

01:42:42.320 --> 01:42:45.380
I know, because we explored it together.

01:43:09.880 --> 01:43:11.530
I...

01:43:13.680 --> 01:43:16.480
I vow to be like that scorpion.

01:43:16.480 --> 01:43:19.490
For the true happiness of everyone...

01:43:19.490 --> 01:43:23.400
I will let my body burn
a hundred times over.

01:43:23.990 --> 01:43:25.920
Campanella...

01:43:30.030 --> 01:43:33.470
No matter where I go,
we'll always be together.

01:45:22.080 --> 01:45:25.630
The phenomenon that is myself

01:45:25.630 --> 01:45:29.430
is one of the imagined organic lights;

01:45:29.430 --> 01:45:32.640
one of its blue illuminations,

01:45:33.640 --> 01:45:38.530
consisting of all kinds of
transparent ghosts.

01:45:40.060 --> 01:45:43.960
While it flickers energetically,

01:45:43.960 --> 01:45:47.570
with the scenery, with everyone,

01:45:47.570 --> 01:45:50.640
It is a blue illumination of a lamp,

01:45:50.640 --> 01:45:56.170
whose alternating currents are
indeed burning very brightly.

01:45:57.660 --> 01:46:02.240
While maintaining the light,
the lamp is lost.

01:46:03.250 --> 01:46:08.690
This is my appreciation of
the past 22 months;

01:46:08.690 --> 01:46:12.840
so I have entered all this on paper,
with mineral ink.

01:46:12.840 --> 01:46:16.930
It all flickers with me,

01:46:16.930 --> 01:46:20.690
everyone can feel it, at the same time.

01:46:21.200 --> 01:46:27.020
A piece of the shadow and the light
that I have kept until now,

01:46:27.020 --> 01:46:30.990
are, as you see, a mental sketch.

01:47:44.700 --> 01:47:51.180
NOW IT BEGINS
